      "math_academy_id": "e-10699",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4z^2}{9}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac {4z^2} {9}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{4z^2}} {\\sqrt{9}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{\\sqrt{3^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{4z^2}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}}}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(2z)^{2}}}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2z|}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{|2|\\cdot |z|}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot |z|}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2|z|}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
